<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gavin Douglas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1474–1522</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gavin Douglas was a Scottish poet and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunkeld">Dunkeld</a>, a son of the Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angus">Angus</a>. His Eneados, a translation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgil">Virgil</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeneid">Aeneid</a> into Scots verse completed in 1513, was the first complete translation of a major classical poem into any form of English. He was also caught up in the faction struggles after Flodden and died in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gavin-douglas.jpg" alt="Gavin Douglas" width="320" height="481" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Stephencdickson · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_of_Gavin_Douglas,_Scottish_National_Portrait_Gallery.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
